00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.869 align:start position:0%
 
world-renowned dancer misty copeland  pays homage to the black ballerinas who  pave the way for her to break barriers  she has written a new book that profiles  27 dancers to inspire the next  generation i'm thrilled to welcome misty  copeland to quick take to talk more  about her latest labor of love black  ballerina misty it's so good to speak  with you  congratulations on your new book this is  your seventh book to be published so far  and i don't know how you find the time  for it but i wonder how long you've been  thinking about planning and kind of  mentally writing this book  yeah this is this is i feel so proud of  this because i feel like it's my my  life's work i feel like my purpose  has been to go beyond the stage and use  my platform and my voice to really  uncover our history as black people in  ballet so this has really been  throughout my journey of of you know  being in ballet since the age of 13  being the only black woman often uh you  know growing up in in the settings of my  training in classical dance and then  joining american ballet theater and  being the only black woman in the  company for the first decade of my  career i feel like it's you know it's  something that i've needed to do to feel  like i belonged and to feel like i was  making the right choices and being a  part of this this art form but it's an  it's incredible to have found so much of  our history that hasn't been documented  as black  as black ballerinas throughout you know  the 400 years that bali has existed yeah  you're trying to connect yourself to  this lineage of black ballerinas and  it's obviously very personal for you you  profile 27 ballerinas is there any one  dancer that you connect with the most  that lingers with you that you think  about when you're dancing when you're  not dancing that you kind of keep coming  back to all the time  oh that's so hard you know 27 i i have  been such a an impact in my life but  they are far from the long lineage of  black dancers like this is not a  comprehensive list but if i had to  choose one you know raven wilkinson who  passed away a couple of years ago was  someone who came into my life a black  ballerina who danced in the 1950s with  the bally rooster onto clock de monte  carlo she was the first black ballerina  to dance in that company and dealt with  a lot of adversity and racism in the  south and the kkk and you know things  that i can't even imagine dealing with  but she came into my life and kind of  gave me a second win to believe that i  did belong on this journey as a  classical dancer and that there was a  much brighter future than i even  imagined but it was having knowing her  story knowing that i was a part of his  history bigger than myself um that gave  me the motivation to keep going that's  great and that's what you bring and give  to the next generation of dancers as  well you talked about how you were the  only black woman when you joined the  american ballet theater abt and for many  years the only black ballerina you were  however surrounded by a lot of male  dancers male black dancers and you saw  the way that they navigated the white  ballet world  why is there such an imbalance first of  all and what did you learn from these  male colleagues  yeah you know it's it's difficult to  find boys that want to participate in  dance um which is mind blowing to me  because it's it's an it's a sport it's  an athletic feat you know we're not  competing like sports but it's that  uh it takes that much uh physical  dedication and commitment and sacrifice  to be a part of but we're just  constantly lacking enough boys so uh the  ballet world has been open to taking  brown and black boys over black women  because we desperately need them um but  it's been  such a big part of my journey to be able  to step back and watch how others  navigate to learn how i want to proceed  i've never been someone that just kind  of like stepped up and and and kind of  spoke before i really understood what  you know the situation of things and  i've really just learned that way by  watching how other people operate and  learning you know it's like just  learning how humans connect and and  interact and that's been one of my  strengths in navigating this very white  world in this very white field  is learning how to communicate with  people in a way where it feels like it's  a it's a conversation and it's not kind  of one-sided or an attack yeah that's  definitely a skill set that you are  forced to develop and obviously you've  done it well  you may not realize this misty but  you're quoted a lot on twitter every few  hours someone quotes you on twitter and  there's a quote attributed to you i mean  i was looking through it and recently a  very popular one is the path to your  success is not as fixed and inflexible  as you think now i'm not sure when you  said this but i'm curious as to how it  applies now  has the pandemic changed your idea of  what success looks like  um yeah it absolutely i think has  changed so much for everyone in the  world uh for me uh it's it's opened up  the arts in a way that i never imagined  it would in my lifetime you know i've  been fighting for more diversity and for  having the conversations about racism in  ballet and including more people making  it more inclusive and within the  pandemic i feel like we grew you know in  leaps and bounds you know just within  the last year and a half by you know  witnessing  the murder of george floyd by having  this racial reckoning the ballet world  has really kind of stepped back and  looked at themselves and thought how can  we improve um and so i think it's  definitely changed how i see the future  of dance and how i see the contributions  of black and brown people within within  this field moving forward i'm so glad  you mentioned that because another quote  that came up a lot is one where you say  be strong  be fearless be beautiful and believe  that anything is possible when you have  the right people there to support you so  in the wake of george floyd and black  lives matter  what kinds of people or institutions or  organizations have stepped up to show  their support  um  you know it's  i what's been surprising for me is  um just people's people's understanding  and hearing things in a different way  uh by having this collective reckoning  so for me it's not so much one person or  institute institution but you know for  instance okay maybe it is an institution  american ballet theater the company i  dance for like i've been very impressed  with how they've stepped up internally  you know and it's not something that  often has been a show when you think  about diversity initiatives especially  in in fields where it's uh you know  majority white and not very diverse you  know it's not just about  putting me on the cover of a pamphlet  and saying okay we've checked our  diversity box but the but the work  that's being done behind the scenes  right now at american ballet theater i'm  so proud to be a part of this company um  the conversations the real conversations  we're having about you know the the the  microaggressions the the the vocabulary  that's being used uh you know ways of  really progressing forward and being  more inclusive um that to me is is the  brightest  thing i could see in terms of how we  move forward in this world and i hope  the burden is not on someone like you to  do the work and show everyone how to get  there as well i mean that's on everyone  to do that i wonder as well given the  pandemic that we're in still a lot of  people have experienced burnout have you  experienced burnout during the pandemic  this has been an interesting time for me  and i'm i'm definitely not you know  the typical person like i feel like i'm  in a very fortunate position for me this  has been a moment for me to step back  and really dive into things i  haven't had time to do  you know being in a classical being in  this field being in classical ballet you  know we have a short careers and there's  not often a lot of time to stop and  really think about  how what else i want to do outside of  performing on stage and so though i've  been fortunate to be able to write books  and  you know speaking engagements and things  like this this has been a moment for me  to really step back and reflect on how i  can uh bring the most value to you know  my reach and my platform um you know  without just being a symbol on the stage  and so this has been a very  um reflective time that i feel like i've  grown in so many ways  uh in seeing myself as a leader not just  on the stage but even more so behind the  scenes and inspiring and impacting  influencing the next generation to be  able to do the work that i'm doing and i  want to pick up on that idea because i  know you think a lot about empowering  the next generation black ballerinas is  a great example of that but the thing is  in professional ballet competition is  cutthroat i mean especially if you're an  underrepresented minority it's easy to  feel that there's only room for one  woman in  the c-suite or one black ballerina  soloist katie couric for instance talked  about how being generous and mentoring  young women younger women um can  sometimes feel like you're grooming your  successor  did you see any evidence of that in your  research for your book  um  i've always  i've always felt very comfortable in  this position so i don't feel like you  know writing this book is kind of the  first time i'm entering into this this  uh  position or feeling this responsibility  of like being a mentor but um i think  it's i think it's you know part of my  responsibility of being in this position  to  uh expose more dancers of color because  it's so it's so empowering when you know  there's more than just you out there  it's something that's maybe not in your  you know that's in your subconscious but  when you think you're the only one it's  even more cutthroat and you really do  believe that there's only one spot but  the more that we support one another  especially as females you know to have a  sisterhood and support one another um  there's just always a better outcome for  me it's been it's never been about how  there being one spot at abt i've done  everything in my power to bring  young brown and black girls up after me  whether it's reaching out and to the  school at american ballet theater trying  to get scholarships for these students  showing the staff that they are out  there and exist that's how we progress  misty fantastic to speak with you

